From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: typec: ucsi: huawei_gaokun: Acknowledge USB events after
 a failed init

The EC reports a port's mode change with a USB event, which the driver
acknowledges. On some boots the EC already has one pending when the
driver registers its notifier, and repeats it about every 200 ms. The
driver acknowledges only once UCSI has registered its connectors: an
event that arrives earlier is left unacknowledged, and the repeats stop
once a later one is handled and acknowledged.

UCSI does not always initialize. On some boots ucsi_init() fails, for
example with "PPM init failed" and -ETIMEDOUT after "failed to register
alt modes". The core then leaves ucsi->connector NULL and does not try
again. If the EC was repeating a USB event, nothing acknowledges it, and
the repeats go on until the next reboot while the driver keeps logging
"ucsi connector is not initialized yet" (rate limited). Each repeat is
an EC interrupt, which wakes the system from suspend, so it cannot stay
asleep for more than a fraction of a second.

Once initialization is over and has failed, acknowledge the event
without handling the port, as the driver already does for an event
without a port update. Initialization counts as over when UCSI is
registered, ucsi->work, which runs ucsi_init() and is queued again only
for -EPROBE_DEFER and -EAGAIN, is neither queued nor running, and
ucsi->connector is still NULL. work_busy() is only a snapshot, so act
only when this holds for two events in a row. While initialization may
still succeed, keep the event unacknowledged as before, so that the mode
of a port whose event arrives early is still applied.

On such a boot the port's mode, orientation and DisplayPort HPD are
still not applied until the next reboot, as before.

Of the failed boots logged so far, two had the EC repeating from boot
and four had no USB event after the failure. A failure simulated by
failing GET_CAPABILITY did not make the EC repeat either. On such a
boot, unplugging and replugging the cable gave two USB events; the first
was only counted and the second was acknowledged, without EC errors.
Whether the acknowledgment alone stops the repeats of a real failed boot
has not been observed.

Fixes: 00327d7f2c8c ("usb: typec: ucsi: add Huawei Matebook E Go ucsi driver")
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi_huawei_gaokun.c | 43 ++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 41 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi_huawei_gaokun.c b/drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi_huawei_gaokun.c
index cb7541cbe55..2433e69ba15 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi_huawei_gaokun.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi_huawei_gaokun.c
@@ -118,6 +118,7 @@ struct gaokun_ucsi {
 	bool registered;
 	bool usb_events; /* the EC has sent a USB event */
 	bool cmd_lost; /* the EC gave up resend_cmd */
+	u8 init_failed_seen; /* USB events seen in a row after a failed init */
 };
 
 /* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@@ -437,12 +438,49 @@ static void gaokun_ucsi_handle_usb_mode(struct gaokun_ucsi_port *port)
 	gaokun_ec_ucsi_pan_ack(uec->ec, port->idx);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Whether UCSI has given up initializing. ucsi_register() only queues
+ * ucsi->work; ucsi_init() runs from it and sets ucsi->connector on success.
+ * On failure it leaves ucsi->connector NULL and does not try again, except
+ * for -EPROBE_DEFER, which queues the work once more. work_busy() is only a
+ * snapshot, so the caller acts on this only when it holds for two USB
+ * events in a row.
+ */
+static bool gaokun_ucsi_init_failed(struct gaokun_ucsi *uec)
+{
+	/* Pairs with smp_store_release() in gaokun_ucsi_register_worker() */
+	return smp_load_acquire(&uec->registered) &&
+	       !work_busy(&uec->ucsi->work.work) &&
+	       !READ_ONCE(uec->ucsi->connector);
+}
+
 /* Returns whether a port had an update */
 static bool gaokun_ucsi_usb_notify_ind(struct gaokun_ucsi *uec)
 {
 	int idx;
 
-	if (!uec->ucsi->connector) { /* slow to register */
+	if (!READ_ONCE(uec->ucsi->connector)) {
+		/*
+		 * On some boots the EC repeats a USB event about every 200 ms
+		 * until it is acknowledged. While UCSI is still initializing,
+		 * leave it unacknowledged: the repeat that arrives once the
+		 * connectors exist is handled in full. Once initialization has
+		 * failed, nothing will handle it, and the repeats would go on
+		 * until reboot, waking the system from suspend each time.
+		 * Acknowledge it then, without handling the port.
+		 */
+		if (!gaokun_ucsi_init_failed(uec))
+			uec->init_failed_seen = 0;
+		else if (uec->init_failed_seen < 2)
+			uec->init_failed_seen++;
+
+		if (uec->init_failed_seen >= 2) {
+			dev_warn_once(uec->dev,
+				      "UCSI failed to initialize, acknowledging USB events without handling them\n");
+			gaokun_ec_ucsi_pan_ack(uec->ec, gaokun_ucsi_refresh(uec));
+			return false;
+		}
+
 		dev_err_ratelimited(uec->dev, "ucsi connector is not initialized yet\n");
 		return false;
 	}
@@ -622,7 +660,8 @@ static void gaokun_ucsi_register_worker(struct work_struct *work)
 		return;
 	}
 
-	uec->registered = true;
+	/* Pairs with smp_load_acquire() in gaokun_ucsi_init_failed() */
+	smp_store_release(&uec->registered, true);
 }
 
 static int gaokun_ucsi_probe(struct auxiliary_device *adev,
